.TH "ITERATOR.ZIP" "3JS" "September 4, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Iterator.zip \- Iterator.zip()
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]Iterator.zip()\f[B]\f[R] static method creates a new
\f[CR]Iterator\f[R] object that aggregates elements from multiple
iterable objects by yielding arrays containing elements at the same
position.
It essentially \(lqzips\(rq the input iterables together, allowing
simultaneous iteration over them.
.PP
The \f[CR]Iterator.zipKeyed()\f[R] method is similar, but yields objects
instead of arrays with keys you can specify.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
Iterator.zip(iterables)
Iterator.zip(iterables, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]iterables\f[R]
An iterable of iterables whose elements are aggregated.
It must be iterable and cannot be an iterator.
It should be finite, although its elements can be infinite iterables.
Each element must implement either the iterable protocol or, failing
that, the iterator protocol.
Strings are rejected: to zip strings, convert them to iterators
explicitly using \f[CR]Iterator.from()\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object specifying behavior in case of inconsistent input lengths.
It may have the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]mode\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
One of the following:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dqshortest\(dq\f[R] (default): The resulting iterator stops when
one input iterable is exhausted.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R]: The resulting iterator stops when all input
iterables are exhausted.
Missing values from shorter iterables are filled according to the
\f[CR]padding\f[R] option.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dqstrict\(dq\f[R]: A \f[CR]TypeError\f[R] is thrown if not all
input iterables finish at the same time.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]padding\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An iterable object (not iterator).
Only retrieved and validated when \f[CR]mode\f[R] is
\f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R].
If \f[CR]undefined\f[R] or absent, missing values from shorter iterables
are filled with \f[CR]undefined\f[R] (which is equivalent to passing an
empty iterable).
If an iterable is provided, it is iterated for the number of times equal
to the number of elements in \f[CR]iterables\f[R] \f[I]as soon as
\f[CI]Iterator.zip()\f[I] is called\f[R].
\f[CR]padding[i]\f[R] is used for missing values for
\f[CR]iterables[i]\f[R] (assuming \f[CR]padding\f[R] and
\f[CR]iterables\f[R] are provided as arrays; they don\(cqt have to be).
If \f[CR]padding\f[R] is shorter than \f[CR]iterables\f[R],
\f[CR]undefined\f[R] is used for the remaining iterables.
.RE
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]Iterator\f[R] object.
Each of its elements is an array with length equal to the number of
input iterables, containing the elements from each input iterable at the
corresponding position.
If the \f[CR]iterables\f[R] object is empty, the resulting iterator is
created as completed.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R] function behaves like a \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpose
transpose
.UE \c
\ operation, yielding arrays containing the elements at matching
positions in each of the inputs.
If we represent iterables as arrays, the input may looks like this:
.IP
.EX
[
  [a1, a2, a3, a4], \f[I]// Iterable a\f[R]
  [b1, b2, b3], \f[I]// Iterable b\f[R]
  [c1, c2, c3, c4, c5], \f[I]// Iterable c\f[R]
];
.EE
.PP
The resulting iterator, regardless of the options, will start by
yielding the following arrays:
.IP
.EX
[a1, b1, c1];
[a2, b2, c2];
[a3, b3, c3];
.EE
.PP
After the first three arrays are yielded, the input iterable
\f[CR]b\f[R] is exhausted on the fourth \f[CR]next()\f[R] call\(emit
returns \f[CR]{ done: true }\f[R].
What happens next depends on the \f[CR]mode\f[R] option.
If \f[CR]mode\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqshortest\(dq\f[R] (the default), the
resulting iterator stops here: the other two input iterators are closed.
If \f[CR]mode\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqstrict\(dq\f[R], an error is thrown
because the other two iterables are \f[I]not\f[R] finished when the
second one yields the result \f[CR]{ done: true }\f[R].
If \f[CR]mode\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R], the resulting iterator
continues yielding arrays, filling missing values.
For example, if \f[CR]padding\f[R] is not provided, it defaults to
\f[CR]undefined\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
[a4, \f[B]undefined\f[R], c4];
[\f[B]undefined\f[R], \f[B]undefined\f[R], c5];
.EE
.PP
If \f[CR]padding\f[R] is provided as an iterable, because there are
three input iterables, the first three values from the
\f[CR]padding\f[R] iterable are used to fill missing values.
Let\(cqs suppose that \f[CR]padding\f[R] is an array with values
\f[CR][p1, p2, p3]\f[R].
Then \f[CR]p2\f[R] is used to fill the missing value from the input
iterable \f[CR]b\f[R], and \f[CR]p1\f[R] is used to fill the missing
value from the input iterable \f[CR]a\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
[a4, p2, c4];
[p1, p2, c5];
.EE
.PP
If the \f[CR]padding\f[R] iterable has fewer than three values, the
remaining missing values are filled with \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Iteration over a map with indices
Using \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R], you can iterate over any iterable
object (strings aren\(cqt supported by default) while also having access
to an incrementing counter:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] ages = \f[B]new\f[R] Map([
  [\(dqCaroline\(dq, 30],
  [\(dqDanielle\(dq, 25],
  [\(dqEvelyn\(dq, 35],
]);

\f[B]const\f[R] numbers = (\f[B]function\f[R]* () {
  \f[B]let\f[R] n = 0;
  \f[B]while\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R]) {
    \f[B]yield\f[R] n++;
  }
})();
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] [index, [name, age]] \f[B]of\f[R] Iterator.zip([numbers, ages])) {
  console.log(\(ga${index}: ${name} is ${age} years old.\(ga);
}

\f[I]// Output:\f[R]
\f[I]// 0: Caroline is 30 years old.\f[R]
\f[I]// 1: Danielle is 25 years old.\f[R]
\f[I]// 2: Evelyn is 35 years old.\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]numbers\f[R] is an infinite iterator that generates incrementing
numbers starting from \f[CR]0\f[R].
Because \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R] stops by default when the shortest
input iterable is exhausted, the loop iterates exactly three times.
The \f[CR]numbers\f[R] iterator is properly closed after the loop ends;
it doesn\(cqt cause an infinite loop.
.SS Creating a Map from lists of keys and values
Suppose you have two arrays: one with keys and another with values.
You can use \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R] to combine them into a
\f[CR]Map\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] days = [\(dqMon\(dq, \(dqTue\(dq, \(dqWed\(dq, \(dqThu\(dq, \(dqFri\(dq];
\f[B]const\f[R] temperatures = [22, 21, 23, 20, 19];

\f[B]const\f[R] dayTemperatureMap = \f[B]new\f[R] Map(Iterator.zip([days, temperatures]));
console.log(dayTemperatureMap);
\f[I]// Map(5) { \(aqMon\(aq => 22, \(aqTue\(aq => 21, \(aqWed\(aq => 23, \(aqThu\(aq => 20, \(aqFri\(aq => 19 }\f[R]
.EE
.SS Joint iteration over multiple data sources
Suppose you have data coming from multiple sources, such as multiple
microservices or databases.
You know that each source provides related data in the same order, and
you want to process them together.
You can use \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R] to achieve this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] names = fetchNames(); \f[I]// e.g., [\(dqCaroline\(dq, \(dqDanielle\(dq, \(dqEvelyn\(dq]\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] ages = fetchAges(); \f[I]// e.g., [30, 25, 35]\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] cities = fetchCities(); \f[I]// e.g., [\(dqNew York\(dq, \(dqLondon\(dq, \(dqHong Kong\(dq]\f[R]

\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] [name, age, city] \f[B]of\f[R] Iterator.zip([names, ages, cities])) {
  console.log(\(ga${name}, aged ${age}, lives in ${city}.\(ga);
}

\f[I]// Output:\f[R]
\f[I]// Caroline, aged 30, lives in New York.\f[R]
\f[I]// Danielle, aged 25, lives in London.\f[R]
\f[I]// Evelyn, aged 35, lives in Hong Kong.\f[R]
.EE
.SS Providing padding for uneven iterables
When zipping iterables of different lengths with \f[CR]mode\f[R] set to
\f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R], you can provide a \f[CR]padding\f[R]
iterable to specify the values used to fill in missing entries:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] letters = [\(dqa\(dq, \(dqb\(dq, \(dqc\(dq, \(dqd\(dq, \(dqe\(dq];
\f[B]const\f[R] numbers = [1, 2, 3];

\f[I]// One padding value per iterable\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] padding = [\(dq[Letter missing]\(dq, \(dq[Number missing]\(dq];
\f[B]const\f[R] it = Iterator.zip([letters, numbers], { mode: \(dqlongest\(dq, padding });
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] [letter, number] \f[B]of\f[R] it) {
  console.log(\(ga${letter}: ${number}\(ga);
}
\f[I]// Output:\f[R]
\f[I]// a: 1\f[R]
\f[I]// b: 2\f[R]
\f[I]// c: 3\f[R]
\f[I]// d: [Number missing]\f[R]
\f[I]// e: [Number missing]\f[R]
.EE
.SS Zipping strings
Strings are not accepted as input iterables to
\f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R], because it is now considered a mistake to
make strings implicitly iterable.
To zip strings, convert them to iterators explicitly using
\f[CR]Iterator.from()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] str1 = \(dqabc\(dq;
\f[B]const\f[R] str2 = \(dq1234\(dq;
\f[B]const\f[R] it = Iterator.zip([Iterator.from(str1), Iterator.from(str2)]);
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] [char1, char2] \f[B]of\f[R] it) {
  console.log(\(ga${char1} \- ${char2}\(ga);
}
\f[I]// Output:\f[R]
\f[I]// a \- 1\f[R]
\f[I]// b \- 2\f[R]
\f[I]// c \- 3\f[R]
.EE
.PP
In some cases, you may wish to split by grapheme clusters instead of
code units.
In that case, you can use the \f[CR]Intl.Segmenter\f[R] API:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] segmenter = \f[B]new\f[R] Intl.Segmenter(\(dqen\-US\(dq, { granularity: \(dqgrapheme\(dq });
\f[B]const\f[R] str1 = \(dq🤷‍♂️🤷‍♀️🤷\(dq;
\f[B]const\f[R] str2 = \(dq123\(dq;
\f[B]const\f[R] it = Iterator.zip([
  segmenter.segment(str1).map(({ segment }) \f[B]=>\f[R] segment),
  segmenter.segment(str2).map(({ segment }) \f[B]=>\f[R] segment),
]);
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] [char1, char2] \f[B]of\f[R] it) {
  console.log(\(ga${char1} \- ${char2}\(ga);
}
\f[I]// Output:\f[R]
\f[I]// 🤷‍♂️ \- 1\f[R]
\f[I]// 🤷‍♀️ \- 2\f[R]
\f[I]// 🤷 \- 3\f[R]
.EE
